{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "2",
    "case_summary": "A 14-year-old boy presented with multiple white, non-itchy areas on his distal extremities for two years. The lesions included whitish papules and depressed areas with central protrusion. On clinical examination, the patient, who appeared otherwise healthy, exhibited lumbar lordosis, laxity in large joints, tibia vara, high-arched palate, and dental misalignment. The skin lesions were scattered, white to skin-colored papules, less than 1 centimeter in diameter, with central protrusion, located on the dorsum of fingers, forearms, distal portion of the thighs, and calves. No lesions were found on the trunk or proximal areas of the extremities. Palms, soles, dorsum of feet, and mucosal membranes were unaffected. There were no sensory changes associated with the lesions. The patient had no history of medication use. Family history was negative. Laboratory tests, including complete blood count, urinalysis, and blood chemistry (erythrocyte sedimentation rate and liver function tests), were within normal limits. Antinuclear antibody was negative. The patient had no risk factors for AIDS or syphilis, so HIV and VDRL tests were not requested. Hepatitis B surface antigens were not detected. Immunological assays for IgM, IgA, IgG, IgE, C3, and C4 levels were normal. A 0.5 centimeter induration was observed after tuberculin testing. Chest x-ray was normal.\n\nHaematoxylin and eosin stained section showed faintly eosinophilic separated collagen fibers in the upper and mid-dermis. Verhoeff-vanGieson stained sections showed a marked decrease or in some areas total absence of elastic fibres, in both superficial and mid-dermis. Elastic fibres around the vessels in the affected areas are also fragmented and markedly decreased.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
